  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeep, closed upper low pressure over eastern Lake Erie tracks \neastward toward New England through the day with moist cyclonic flow \nmaintained in its wake across Southeast MI. Morning stratus evolves \ninto stratocumulus as daytime mixing commences, with mostly cloudy \nskies prevailing through the day. Scattered early morning showers \nwill move out leaving dry conditions the rest of the day save for a \nspotty sprinkle or two. The cold core of the system has already \ndeparted and will allow for high temps to rebound slightly compared \nto yesterday, landing in the lower 50s. A tight corridor of \nisentropic downglide accompanies the arrival of anticyclonic flow \nthis afternoon, leading to rapid drying and the eventual clearing of \nremaining clouds this evening. Favorable radiating conditions cause \ntemperatures to quickly drop this evening, settling into the lower \n30s and upper 20s overnight.\n\nA Pacific jet streak arriving into the PNW causes the next Canadian \nshortwave to amplify as it tracks from southern Manitoba into the \nGreat Lakes on Sunday. This wave induces a period of warm air \nadvection on breezy southwesterly wind Sunday morning. Temperatures \nrebound into the mid 50s while afternoon wind becomes gusty to \naround 30 mph. The attendant surface low passes northeast of \nGeorgian Bay late Sunday, dragging a cold front across the area. The \ngreater lift tied to upper height falls and PVA will focus over Lake \nHuron, but a slight chance (20%) of light showers will exist along \nand behind the front Sunday evening.\n\nCold air advection behind the Sunday wave is short-lived and \ntemperatures will stay on the mild side into the mid-week. A \nprogressive upper air pattern persists through this period with a \nshortwave ridge cresting overhead Monday before the next trough \narrives on Tuesday. Fropa tied to this wave also looks mostly dry as \nthe bulk of forcing slides north of the local area. By the end of \nthe week, more persistent troughing develops across the Great Lakes \nand eastern CONUS. This will favor temperatures falling on the \ncooler side of average. Northerly flow shows potential for lake \neffect rain and snow showers at times.",
